{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 27, :comments ["<b>Therefore the mashal-sayers say.</b> Those who compose meshalim from their own heart — they were Balaam and Beor his father, according to the opinion of the Rabbis, blessed be their memory, for they would deliver meshalim about future events, and they would say 'Come to Heshbon' — for Sihon could not conquer it; he went and hired Balaam to curse it, and this is what Balak said (Numbers 22): 'For I know that he whom you bless is blessed, etc.' <b>Let it be built and established</b> — in the name of Sihon, to be his city." "<b>And the Rabbis interpreted</b>, blessed be their memory, in the chapter 'HaMokher et haSefinah' (Bava Batra 78a): <b>Therefore the mashal-sayers say</b> — these are those who rule over their inclination. <b>Come to Heshbon</b> — come and let us reckon the account of the world: the loss of a mitzvah against its reward, and the reward of a transgression against its loss. If you do so — <b>let it be built and established</b>: you build in this world and establish for the world to come. <b>The city of Sihon</b> — if a person makes himself like a city, this one who follows after fine conversation (sikhah). What is written after it? <b>For a fire went forth from Heshbon</b> — fire goes forth from those who reckon (meḥashvim) and consumes those who do not reckon. <b>And a flame</b> from the city of the righteous, who were called Sihon, as it is written (Judges 5:10): ״והולכי על דרך שיחו״. <b>It consumed Ar of Moab</b> — this one who follows after his inclination, like a city — this one who follows after fine conversation (sikhah). <b> Baalei Bamot Arnon</b> — these are those who immerse [i.e., force] their minds, as the Master said: whoever forces his mind is like one who worships idolatry. <b>And their yoke (weniram)</b> — said the wicked man: there is none exalted (ram). <b>Heshbon is lost</b> — the account of the world is lost. <b>As far as Dibon</b> — wait until judgment (din) comes. <b>And as far as Nophah</b> — until a fire goes forth that needs no blowing (nifuch). <b>As far as Medeva</b> — until their souls speak (yitdavenah). And some say: until he does what he wants."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {1 "The closing phrase עד שתדוב נשמתן ('until their souls speak') is a play on דיבור/דיבה; the Aramaic variant עד דעבד מאי דבעי ('until he does what he wants') plays on מידבא/דעבד. Both rendered hyper-literally; the wordplay does not carry into English."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:34:48.528698741Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}